# --- s013 Book I d.2 q.1 (= Ord. I d.2 p.1 qq.1–2)
n("n279","s013-01","Vna realis scilicet vtrum sit in entibus aliquid actu infinitum. Secunda vtrum deum esse sit per se notum.","la","source","= *Ord.* I d. 2 p. 1 q. 1 (n. 1: *utrum in entibus sit aliquid exsistens actu infinitum*) and q. 2 (n. 10: *utrum aliquod infinitum sit per se notum, ut Deum esse*).","verified")
n("n280","s013-01","⟨mouet in⟩","la","textual","A is obscured by a stain at the top of the page. The words are supplied from B (*nulla autem mouet in non tempore*); likewise ⟨um 8°⟩, where B has *Ad oppositum. viij. phisicorum*.","verified")
n("n281","s013-01","8° phisicorum si esset","la","source","Aristotle, *Phys.* VIII.10, 266a24–b6 = *Ord.* I d. 2 n. 6. The *ad oppositum* (266a10–24) is n. 7.","verified")
n("n282","s013-02","respondet quod maior est vera etc.","la","source","*Ord.* I d. 2 n. 2: *Respondetur quod maior est vera de contrariis formaliter*. Gorris identifies the anonymous respondent as William of Ware (*In I Sent.* d. 2 q. 1).","verified")
n("n283","s013-02","sed non est de textu","la","textual","Gorris notes that some manuscripts insert a further question, *Utrum sit aliquod ens simpliciter primum*, but rejects it as ‘not of the text’. This is an early witness to the manuscript variation in *Ord.* I d. 2 (cf. the Vatican edition’s *textus interpolatus*).","certain")
n("n284","s013-02","prima parte summe. q. 2. articulo. 1.","la","source","Thomas, *ST* I q. 2 a. 1 (*per se notum secundum se / quoad nos*); and I-II q. 94 a. 2 (*quaedam per se nota solis sapientibus*). B agrees (*q. ij. ar. j.*; *q. xciiij. ar. ij.*). Scotus rejects these distinctions in *Ord.* I d. 2 nn. 20–25.","verified")
n("n285","s013-02","li. 1. dis. 2. q. 8.","la","source","William of Ware, *In I Sent.* d. 2 q. 8 (per se nota of first and second order). Not verified.","to-verify")
n("n286","s013-03","Henricus quodlibeto. 9° q. 1. et 2.","la","source","Henry of Ghent, *Quodl.* IX qq. 1–2 (on the divine ideas and exemplar causality). Cf. *Ord.* I d. 2 n. 41 ff.","to-verify")
n("n287","s013-03","primo scripti ex distinctione. 35. q. 1.","la","source","Thomas, *Scriptum in I Sent.* d. 35 q. 1 (a. 1?), on God’s knowledge. B: *primo scripto distinctione xxxv*?","to-verify")
n("n288","s013-03","Egidius de roma quodlibeto. 3°","la","source","Giles of Rome, *Quodl.* III. B: *Egidius de rho. quol. iij.*","to-verify")
n("n289","s013-03","Alexander de Alexandria de ordine minorum officio generalis","la","historical","Alexander of Alessandria OFM (d. 1314), minister general 1313–14, commentator on the *Sentences* (d. 35 q. 2). Gorris traces Scotus’s fifth proof (*Item quinto posset sic argui*) to him.","to-verify")
n("n290","s013-04","Henrici quodlibeto. 4. q. vlt.","la","source","Henry of Ghent, *Quodl.* IV (last question); Richard of Middleton, *In I Sent.* d. 2 a. 4 q. 4. Both argue for infinity from the infinite distance between the extremes of creation; Scotus rejects this in *Ord.* I d. 2 n. 112 ff.","to-verify")
n("n291","s013-04","prima parte summe. q. 7. arti. 1.","la","source","Thomas, *ST* I q. 7 a. 1: form is limited by matter, so God, pure form, is infinite. Scotus criticizes this in *Ord.* I d. 2 n. 137.","verified")
n("n292","s013-05","Scoti dicitur ad secundam questionem","la","textual","The running headline of p. 25 reads *Di.i. Q.i* for *Di.ii. Q.i*: a misprint of the running headline.","verified")
n("n293","s013-05","per Aug. 1. metha. c. 1.","la","textual","A *Aug.* is an error for Avicenna. B reads *per Auicenam .i. metaphisice capitulo .i.*, which is correct: Avicenna, *Metaph.* I c. 1, says God’s existence is proved in metaphysics.","verified")
n("n294","s013-05","sunt per se note propositiones","la","source","Cf. *Ord.* I d. 2 n. 26: the proposition is *per se nota* in which the extremes, as they are naturally apt to be conceived, evidently cause the truth. Gorris’s next sentence paraphrases n. 23.","certain")
n("n295","s013-06","Prima quod est aliquod primum ineffectibile","la","source","The *triplex primitas* of *Ord.* I d. 2 nn. 43–58 (first effective being: n. 43 *aliquod effectivum est simpliciter primum*; nn. 57–58 *incausabile*, *actu exsistens*). B reads *ineffabile*, a corruption of *ineffectibile*.","verified")
n("n296","s013-07","quatuor viis","la","source","The four ways to infinity in *Ord.* I d. 2 nn. 111–147: efficiency (n. 111), intellect (n. 115), end (n. 128), eminence (n. 137).","verified")
n("n297","s013-08","Scotus videtur quod contradicat sibi ipsi","la","commentary","This is the *contradictio* between *Ord.* I d. 2 n. 26 (*Deus est* is *per se nota*) and n. 27 ff. (it is not known *per se* to us). The first solution (intuitive vs abstractive cognition) was common among early Scotists.","certain")
n("n298","s013-08","⟨filius⟩","la","textual","A is stained; B reads *Sed filius cuiusdam soluit sic*. Who is meant by the ‘son of a certain [master]’ (a pupil, *filius* in the Order?) is unknown.","uncertain")
n("n299","s013-08","quia potest probari a priori","la","textual","So A and B. The sense seems to require *non potest*, or else ‘can be proved’ means by demonstration *quia*.","uncertain")
n("n300","s013-05","by Avicenna","en","translation","Translating B’s reading; A has *Aug.*")
n("n301","s013-06","which cannot be produced","en","translation","*ineffectibile*: Scotus’s term for what cannot be the effect of anything.")
n("n302","s013-08","the son of a certain [master]","en","translation","*filius cuiusdam*, perhaps a disciple. The identification is unresolved.")
# --- B collation (full): B24L–B26L
n("n1057","s013-02","discussa propositionis","la","textual","B: *subtilissime discussiua propositionis per se note*.","certain")
n("n1058","s013-02","nulla est distinctio per se nota","la","textual","B: *nulla est distinctio propositionis per se nota in se et nota nature et nobis*. B's added *propositionis* makes the construction clearer.","certain")
n("n1059","s013-02","idem faciebat Thomas ibidem ex prima secunde","la","textual","B: *idem Tho. faciebat ibidem parte prima secunde*.","certain")
upd("n287",text="Thomas, *Scriptum in I Sent.*, on God's knowledge. B: *primo scripto distinctione xiiij.* (or *xiij.*; the numeral is clear only as far as *xii…*) *q. j.*, against A's *35*. Neither reference has been checked against Thomas.")
n("n1060","s013-04","est distantia infinita","la","textual","B: *est distinctio* (without *infinita*). A gives the argument its point.","certain")
upd("n295",append="B: *primum ineffabile*, an evident misprint for Scotus's *ineffectibile*.")
n("n1061","s013-06","potest esse accidens prime nature","la","textual","B: *potest esse accipiens prime nature*, a misprint; A is right.","certain")
n("n1062","s013-07","omnia scibilia et sic","la","textual","B: *omnia scibilia et simul* ('and at once'), which suits the following *vnico actu*. A is construable ('and so on'), and the translation keeps it.","certain")
n("n1063","s013-08","dicit quod deus est. est per se nota","la","textual","B: *dicit quod illa deus est est per se nota*.","certain")
n("n1064","s013-08","conclusi⟨one locu⟩tus","la","textual","A is stained here; only *conclu-* and *-tus est* are legible. Supplied from B, *in prima conclusione locutus est de noticia intuitiua*. (An earlier draft supplied *Scotus*, which B does not support.)","certain")
n("n1065","s013-09","agit ex necessitate nature agit in non tempore","la","textual","B: *agit ex necessitate nature vel voluntate.* B omits *agit in non tempore. sed deus non agit necessitate nature sed*, a skip from *nature* to *nature*. A is right.","certain")
